    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Terrestrial carbon cycle modeling",
    "problem": "Current models of terrestrial carbon dynamics poorly characterize dryland ecosystems because they do not explicitly account for the tight coupling between water availability and carbon uptake, thus underestimating the large seasonal and interannual variability of the dryland carbon sink.",
    "method": "We propose DryFlux, a machine learning model that fuses in situ carbon flux measurements with remote sensing and meteorological data to improve predictions of dryland gross primary productivity by explicitly accounting for water availability.",
    "task": "The task is to improve dryland carbon uptake predictions by developing a machine learning product, DryFlux, that fuses on-site measurements with remote sensing and climate data, explicitly accounting for the crucial link between water availability and the carbon cycle.",
